PUBLIC RESPONSE: {D

The ESA rules tie the obligation to prepare and maintain both the disconnecting-from-work policy and the electronic-monitoring policy to the headcount on January 1 of the prior year; once that 25-employee threshold is met, the duty continues for the following calendar year regardless of later reductions. Employer D had 30 employees on 1 January 2022, so both policies had to be in place and distributed by the statutory deadlines in 2022. The absence of any electronic-monitoring policy therefore leaves D non-compliant on 2 January 2023.

The claim that C necessarily violates the 30-day distribution rule is weaker than it appears. Monthly updates followed by quarterly distribution could still satisfy the statute if each revision is handed out within 30 days of its effective date (for example, if the January revision is circulated in early February, the February revision in early March, etc.). Nothing in the facts shows that any particular revision missed the 30-day window, so C’s schedule does not on its face breach the ESA. By contrast, D’s outright failure to adopt the required electronic-monitoring policy is an unambiguous and continuing violation. All remaining options either stayed below the threshold on every relevant January 1 date or already satisfy the policy and distribution requirements.}

