7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {The strongest arguments against D overlook how the ESA's annual threshold actually operates. The 25-employee test is performed on January 1 of the triggering year (here, 2022 for D), and once that line is crossed the duty to adopt and keep both the disconnecting-from-work policy and the electronic-monitoring policy in force attaches for the entire compliance cycle that follows. D had 30 employees on 1 January 2022, so both policies were required throughout 2022; the complete absence of any electronic-monitoring policy is therefore an ongoing breach that remains live on 2 January 2023.  

Headcount reductions later in 2022 do not erase that obligation mid-cycle or automatically reset it for the start of 2023. The statute does not contain a “look-back” forgiveness clause; compliance is measured against the policy that should have existed from the moment the threshold was met.  

By contrast, the objections to C rest on an unproven assumption that its quarterly distribution schedule must have missed the 30-day window. Nothing in the facts shows any specific revision was delivered late; monthly updates could still be circulated within 30 days if each one is handed out promptly after its effective date. That leaves C factually compliant on the record before us, while D’s total omission of one required policy is unambiguous. The result is that only D is demonstrably out of compliance as of the question date.}

